  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 1236 PM CDT Sat Aug 8 2026\n\nNear term Through Sunday...\nQuiet and less humid this afternoon with brief intrusion of high \npressure. Fair weather cu as high temps peak in the upper 70s to \nlower 80s. Late tonight, attention will be west over southern MN \nwhere convergence on nose of low- level jet and warm air advection\noccurs on the edge of steeper mid-level lapse rates/MUCAPEs. \nComplex of showers/storms is signaled to occur on nose of the LLJ \nas it shifts into WI late tonight/Sunday morning.\n\nBased on probabilities of 0.01/0.05 inch of 6hr QPF from \nensembles, expect the main bulk of showers/storms to track over \nsouthern WI closer to gradient of instability. However, additional\nshowers and isolated storms will occur farther north into our \narea. Will have to watch to see if the complex is slower to \ndevelop as that would open door to intensification as it moves \ninto especially our southern areas, south of highway 10 late \nSunday morning. If that occurred, there would be at least a small \nrisk for gusty winds and/or heavy rain. A couple of the CAMs point\nto this idea. Another area of showers will be present over far \nnorthern WI closer to upper level jet and sfc front. Highs on \nSunday will be held down due to the clouds and rain, mainly in the\n70s. If rain ends up more widespread, could even see some \nlocations staying in the lower 70s. Not going that cool yet \nthough.\n\nRain/storm chances rest of the forecast...\nNo big changes to going forecast as prevailing northwest flow \nresults in several embedded shortwaves that bring at least the \nopportunity for chances of showers/storms through the better part \nof next week. Now appears main focus for rain on Sunday night and \nMonday morning could end up farther south, though with building \ninstability on Monday afternoon, will see slight uptick in \nshower/storm potential over central and east-central WI as sfc \ntrough crosses the region even as main front will be over southern\nWI/northern IL into lower MI. Pops not all that high though at \n15-30%, so probably just looking at isolated activity. Shear would\nbe sufficient to have some severe weather risk though. SPC \ncurrently has the Day3 Marginal (level 1 of 5) to our south and \nWPC slight risk for excessive rainfall is also south. \n\nNext potential for rain would be later Monday night into Tuesday \nmorning, though with low-level jet and frontal boundary farther\nsouth, imagine greatest chances for storms of consequence will be\nfarther south as well. Similar to Monday though, if enough instability\nmaterializes during peak heating, could see smaller chances for storms\non Tuesday afternoon. That pattern could repeat yet again on \nWednesday before the frontal boundary moves far enough south to \nbring dry weather in for late this week. \n\nTemperatures...\nBeyond the cooler readings Sunday, high temperatures Monday \nthrough Wednesday should be as warm as the lower 80s, though any \nmorning convection these days could alter those expectations. Late\nnext week, northwest flow and troughing increases leading to \ncooler temps in the lower to middle 70s for most locations. Low \ntemperatures could drop into the 40s over northern WI by late next\nweek.",
